  "summary": "Recent preprints and a new tool reprocess existing RNA-seq and Ribo-Seq data to adjudicate disputed transcript structures and coding claims.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is adjudicating transcript structure and coding claims by reprocessing existing experimental evidence rather than accepting catalogue assignments. An improved TEChim reanalysis argues that transposon insertions act as alternative exons in Drosophila brain transcripts, explicitly rebutting an earlier study. InspectorORF applies a similar evidence-first approach to novel open reading frames, displaying Ribo-Seq reads alongside RNA-seq, proteomic and long-read data for validation. A coefficient-of-variation filter for RNA-seq, tested on integrated bulk and snRNA-seq Arabidopsis data, addresses the upstream question of which low-expressed genes are retained for analysis. The human genome reannotation, which classifies genes as coding, non-coding or bifunctional and reports roughly 4000 bifunctional genes with a searchable database, is more catalogue-oriented and sits at the edge of this thread. What is new is the emphasis on reinspecting existing evidence to resolve annotation disputes.",
